{# User message for the `mock` command.

   Chunks are labelled with their citation rather than pasted in raw. The
   label is what makes grounding checkable: the model has been handed the
   exact string it must reproduce in the `source` and `page` fields, so a
   fabricated citation becomes obvious rather than plausible. #}
Course: {{ course }}
{% if topic %}Focus: {{ topic }}
{% endif %}
Write {{ count }} practice exam questions from the material below.

MATERIAL
{% for chunk in chunks %}
[{{ chunk.citation }}]
{% set source_context = chunk.source_context %}
{% if source_context %}SOURCE STRUCTURE (metadata, not additional evidence):
{{ source_context | tojson }}
A figure context identifies extracted picture text. Spatial relationships are not reconstructed;
isolated axis labels or figure fragments are not standalone prose and do not establish missing relationships.
{% endif %}{{ chunk.text }}

{% endfor %}
END OF MATERIAL

Write up to {{ count }} questions drawn from the passages above.

For each question set exactly these fields:
  topic           two to five words naming what is being tested
  question        positive, self-contained, single-best-answer stem
  options         one object with exactly four keys: A, B, C, and D
  correct_option  exactly one of A, B, C, or D

Each A-D option must be an object with exactly these fields:
  text         the option shown to the student
  explanation  concise feedback stating why it is correct or what is wrong,
               including the corrected knowledge
  source       the course-relative file name shown in brackets, exactly as written
  page         the first cited page number
  page_end     the final cited page number for a range, otherwise null

Every option needs its own citation. The citation for an incorrect option supports
the corrected knowledge in its explanation, not the false distractor wording.
Do not add top-level answer/source/page fields or a difficulty field.

Spread the questions across different passages. Do not write two questions
that test the same point. If a passage is administrative, a citation, or a
fragment of a table, skip it.
